# Target C: outer isolate blockers fit in defect-sized boundary collars

Date: 2026-10-05. Base main: `0351c2c3aecd34c0427d0ad081afae563468d401`.
State: `2026-10-05.audit.61`.
Classification: **proved candidate awaiting independent audit**; artifact
lifecycle **unreviewed**. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Exact implication

```
actual small O--O moves on a near-disk full-core source
-> keep pocket isolates fixed, dispatch only extreme exterior isolates,
   transport bounded-face isolates with their common owner
-> every outer-isolate lower witness lies in a pocket/cap boundary collar
-> total collar population is O(x_B+m_B)
-> k<=150(x_B+m_B) for upper-safe, rhombus-safe shortening singletons
-> no linear usable owner family if x_B+m_B=o(D).
```

This eliminates total outer-isolate population as independent currency
for the stated move scope. It does not extract a linear O--O family,
prove unary/upper safety, or close the separate container branch.

Dependencies: canonical Section 67 convex pockets and perimeter identities;
Section 77 actual exterior caps; Type-II near-disk force hull from Section
80; audit.60 long-pair isolate dispatch; audit.61 actual O--O bounded-face
transport. The final owner count uses the **unreviewed candidate** loaded
incidence theorem in `target_c_loaded_neighbor_outer_owner_bound_2026-10-05.md`.
No exterior Reuleaux containment or nonconsecutive-grid shield is used.

## 2. Pocket perimeter is controlled by outer-walk excess

Let F=conv B=conv V be the force hull, q=|B|, b the occurrence-counted
outer contact-walk length, and x_B=b-q. Let a=m_B be the number of nonunit
loaded-hull sides, and L_add their total length. They are precisely the
missing sides added in canonical hull augmentation: a unit side already
is an actual contact. The canonical finite identities are

    P_F=q-a+L_add,
    sum pocket perimeters=b+2L_add-P_F.

The outer walk has length at least P_F. Hence L_add<=x_B+a and

    sum pocket perimeters=x_B+a+L_add<=2(x_B+a).        (2.1)

There are a convex pockets. Their populations include all outer-contact-
face isolates inside F, with isolates on added hull sides assigned to
their adjacent interior pocket. Bounded physical-face isolates are a
separate population.

## 3. Actual exterior caps have the same perimeter scale

In Type II center coordinates so that B(0,r_D) is contained in F and
r_D/D tends to 1/2. Every source point z satisfies |z|+r_D<=D, because
its distance to the force-hull point -r_D*z/|z| is at most D. Thus

    K=conv A is contained in B(0,R_D), R_D=D-r_D,
    alpha_D=acos(r_D/R_D) tends to zero.

Each canonical exterior cap is bounded by a force-hull chord with
outward unit normal u and the corresponding full-source hull arc. The
chord is at distance at least r_D from zero. A point z of that arc has
u dot z>=r_D and |z|<=R_D, so its radial angle differs from u by at most
alpha_D. Every supporting normal n of K there also satisfies n dot z>=r_D,
so its angle from z is at most alpha_D. The arc's tangent directions
therefore differ from the chord tangent by at most 2alpha_D.
Projection onto that tangent gives, for chord length L,

    arc length<=L/cos(2alpha_D),
    cap perimeter<=(1+sec(2alpha_D))*L<=3L             (3.1)

for all sufficiently large sources along the Type-II sequence.

No positive-area cap can have a unit chord for large D. Its arc would
contain an intermediate source-hull vertex and have at least two sides,
each of length at least one. But the projection bound gives arc length
at most sec(2alpha_D)<2 for a unit chord. Therefore positive caps occur
only on the a nonunit sides, and

    number of positive caps<=a,
    sum cap perimeters<=3L_add<=3(x_B+a).              (3.2)

This is a direct bound for actual K-versus-F caps. It asserts no location
inside a Reuleaux minor cap and supplies no packing sites by area averaging.
Together (2.1)--(3.2) give total region perimeter at most 5(x_B+a),
for at most 2a pocket/cap regions.

## 4. Isolate assignment and lower-witness location

Take the stated actual small O--O binary family and its checked long-pair
root dispatch. Its displacement cloud, including zero, must satisfy the
canonical bound s<min(D-sqrt(D^2-1),sqrt(2)-1). Use this full-source
assignment:

- every bounded-face isolate moves with its common core owner;
- every outer-pocket isolate inside F stays fixed;
- exterior isolates receive canonical long-pair anchors, with unanchored
  ones fixed.

An isolate in F has no strict-long pair. Represent it by source points
of B and apply the variance identity: all those points are at least one
from it, giving every old distance at most sqrt(D^2-1). Keeping a pocket
isolate fixed is therefore upper-safe. The canonical bounded-face proof
makes its transported isolates lower- and upper-safe. Every moving
exterior isolate is a strict-long graph endpoint and hence an extreme
point of K; only those endpoints receive nontrivial dispatch anchors.
The original exception set remains explicit and is handled separately.

Any lower equality/violation comes from an old noncontact pair of length
at most 1+s<5/4 for sufficiently large D. Audit.61 puts all isolate
witnesses in the outer contact face. Consider an isolate endpoint z:

- If z is in a pocket, it is fixed. The other endpoint must move and
  cannot be another isolate of that pocket, all of which are fixed.
  No core vertex lies in its open pocket. The other moving endpoint is
  outside the pocket or on its boundary. Thus z is within 5/4 of the
  boundary of its assigned pocket.
- If z is outside F in a cap and moves, it is on K's boundary, hence on
  the boundary of that cap. If z is fixed, the other endpoint either
  is outside its cap, or is a moving exterior isolate on the cap's hull
  arc. Core points lie in F; other exterior moving isolates lie on K;
  pocket isolates are fixed. In every case z is within 5/4 of its cap
  boundary.

Bounded-face isolates cannot be witness endpoints under the canonical
transport proof. The argument applies to every bad binary local state,
not merely singleton selections. Consequently all isolate endpoints of
lower clauses belong to these collars, irrespective of the full number
of isolates deeper in the pockets or caps.

## 5. Exact collar population estimate

For a convex region of perimeter P, its radius-r boundary tube has area
at most 2rP+pi*r^2. The outer parallel body contributes rP+pi*r^2;
inner erosion loses at most rP by integrating its nonincreasing convex
perimeter, including the case of an empty erosion.

Let N be the number of distinct source isolates in the radius-5/4 collars
of all assigned pockets/caps. Their open radius-1/2 disks are disjoint
and lie in the radius-7/4 tubes. With A_*=x_B+a, (2.1)--(3.2) give

    (pi/4)N <= 2*(7/4)*5A_* + pi*(7/4)^2*2a,
    N <= (70/pi)A_*+(49/2)a < 48A_*.                 (5.1)

The final rational bound uses pi>3 and a<=A_*. The estimate counts
boundary-near source points, not available capacity. Tube disks may
extend outside their regions; this only enlarges the area upper bound.

## 6. Composition with actual safe singleton proposals

Assume every surviving singleton preserves all old contacts, keeps old
diameters at most D and old nondiameters strictly below D, passes all
unary rhombus tests, and strictly shortens an old diameter. Canonical
normalization forces an outer-face lower witness for every owner.

The candidate loaded-neighbor theorem gives at most two loaded neighbors
within 5/4 of any source point for large D, and charges loaded-loaded
noncontact witnesses to the a nonunit loaded-hull sides. Its exact endpoint
count can therefore use only the active collar isolates of (5.1):

    k<=2a+3x_B+3N<=147(x_B+a)<150(x_B+m_B).            (6.1)

Use a non-strict final bound k<=150(x_B+m_B), including the zero-defect
case where there are no regions or isolate witnesses. If canonical
upper-exception deletion removes at most 2d_H owners from an original
r-family and all the remaining singleton hypotheses hold, then

    r<=150(x_B+m_B)+2d_H.                            (6.2)

Thus actual r=Omega(D) safe proposals are impossible when
x_B+m_B+d_H=o(D). In the cyclic diameter branch d_H=0 the upper-exception
term vanishes. This is a conditional exclusion of that finite-motion
subcase, not proof that every cyclic survivor has such proposals.

In particular, a large outer-isolate population cannot rescue the
low-outer-excess near-disk branch: only its defect-sized boundary collar
can block the selected motions under this assignment. The old pocket
area can still contain many fixed isolates; they are all retained.

## 7. Remaining scopes and validation

Actual O--O extraction, source contact preservation under every binary
selection, unary rhombus immunity and the active/inactive upper tests
remain required. A physical pentagon-to-pentagon train family is not
an O--O proposal family. The near-disk force-hull hypothesis is essential
to the exterior-cap estimate; the macroscopic-container branch has not
been covered by it. No new site, net safe-area credit or finite pivot
termination is inferred from collar geometry.

All proofs are analytic finite incidence, convex geometry and packing
counts. No numerical verifier or global-optimality computation is claimed.
Required repository checks validate metadata/state consistency; the new
result and the cited loaded-incidence candidate await independent audit.
